<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · France and the Low Countries</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Bidache</h1><p class="st-meta">1570 CE – 1790 CE (approx.) · capital Bidache</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A tiny sovereign lordship on the edge of the Basque Country whose Gramont lords claimed independence from France until the Revolution.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bidache">Bidache</a>, on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bidouze">Bidouze</a> river between <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/viscounty-of-bearn">Béarn</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-navarre">Navarre</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/labourd">Labourd</a>, belonged to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-gramont">Gramont family</a>. Exploiting its uncertain position on the frontier of Navarre and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, the Gramonts asserted full <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sovereignty">sovereignty</a> over it, and the register dates the principality from 1570.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-gramont">Gramonts</a> ruled the small territory from their castle at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bidache">Bidache</a>, administering justice and taxation without royal interference, while serving the French kings at court and in war. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/antoine-iii-de-gramont">Antoine III de Gramont</a> was a marshal of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and diplomat under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>. The lands were rural, with Gascon and Basque speakers.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>The Revolution abolished the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-gramont">Gramonts</a>' privileges, and in 1790 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bidache">Bidache</a> was incorporated into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and the new <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basses-pyrenees">Basses-Pyrénées</a>. The ruined castle remains.</p><p class="st-conf">Few sources survive for this state; details are uncertain.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-bidache.jpg" alt="Principality of Bidache" width="320" height="204"><figcaption>Image: Mortadelo2005 · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Canton_de_Bidache.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1570 CE – 1790 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Bidache</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Antoine III de Gramont</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholic Christianity</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Gascon and Basque</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/france">France</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Sovereignty of Bidache</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1680&amp;focus=principality-of-bidache" aria-label="Principality of Bidache on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4653 636 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Bidache lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M4973 892L5054 728"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5054 728h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4973 892h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4973 892h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Bidache. <a href="/atlas?year=1680&amp;focus=principality-of-bidache">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
